      <section id="research" aria-labelledby="research-heading">
        <h2 class="section-heading" id="research-heading">Research</h2>

        <article class="content-row media-right">
          <div class="entry-copy">
            <p>
              <strong class="entry-title"
                >FLARE. FunctionaL Annotation and REgularized Sparse Matrix Factorization for GWAS Summary Statistics</strong
              >
            </p>
            <p>
              GWAS have generated extensive summary statistics across complex traits. Matrix factorization provides a natural way to summarize a
              high-dimensional variant-by-trait association matrix in terms of a smaller number of latent genetic factors. FLARE is designed to
              incorporate functional annotations directly into the factorization.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-meta">LASSO · Graph Laplacian · Factor Analysis</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="square-media project-art">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/flare-manhattan-cover.jpg' | relative_url }}?v=20260722"
              alt="GWAS Manhattan plot connected to functional-annotation tracks and latent factor matrices"
              width="600"
              height="600"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </figure>
        </article>

        <article class="content-row media-left">
          <figure class="square-media project-art ukraine-cover">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/ukraine-youth-cover.jpg' | relative_url }}"
              alt="Ukrainian children traveling by train after fleeing the 2022 Russian invasion"
              width="960"
              height="640"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </figure>
          <div class="entry-copy">
            <p><strong class="entry-title">The Ukraine Youth and Family Longitudinal Study</strong></p>
            <p>
              This study follows 1,200 youth and 1,200 parents/caregivers, tracking their mental health, daily experiences, exposure to stress, and
              patterns of adaptation. As an analyst, I applied dyadic analysis to examine associations in war-related psychological distress within
              Ukrainian adolescent–parent dyads.
            </p>
            <p>
              <a
                class="quiet-link"
                href="https://sites.brown.edu/globalflourishing/research/youth-war-displacement/uls/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
                >Learn more</a
              >
            </p>
            <p class="entry-meta">
              <a class="quiet-link" href="https://davidakenny.net/kkc/kkc.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dyadic analysis</a> · Logistic GEE Model
            </p>
            <p class="entry-credit">
              Cover photo:
              <a
                class="quiet-link"
                href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ukrainian_children_are_fleeing_Russian_aggression._Przemy%C5%9Bl,_Poland_27_02_2022_(51912279462).jpg"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
                >Mirek Pruchnicki</a
              >
              · <a class="quiet-link" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/" target="_blank" rel="license noopener">CC BY 2.0</a> ·
              cropped for display
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
