      <section id="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-heading">
        <h2 class="section-heading" id="projects-heading">Projects</h2>

        <h3 class="project-subheading">Graduate</h3>

        <article class="content-row media-left">
          <figure class="square-media project-art">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/abm-seir-cover.jpg' | relative_url }}"
              alt="Agent network with four epidemic states above a family of Monte Carlo trajectories"
              width="600"
              height="600"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </figure>
          <div class="entry-copy">
            <p><strong class="entry-title">ABM–SEIR Simulation Platform</strong></p>
            <p>
              What changes when people stop behaving like identical dots in an equation? This Shiny app lets individual agents meet, move through
              S–E–I–R states, and generate many possible epidemic curves. Run Monte Carlo scenarios, compare interventions, and watch small
              behavioural assumptions become population-level outcomes. The agents never cough; the confidence intervals still get busy.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-meta">Agent-based modelling · SEIR · Monte Carlo simulation</p>
            <p class="entry-links">
              <a class="quiet-link" href="https://zhongyu.shinyapps.io/2560FinalProject/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Site</a>
              ·
              <a class="quiet-link" href="https://github.com/fan-zhongyu/ABM-SEIR-Simulation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <h3 class="project-subheading">Undergraduate</h3>

        <article class="content-row media-left">
          <figure class="square-media project-art signal-cover">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/the-life-of-a-signal-cover.jpg' | relative_url }}"
              alt="Black-and-white illustration of a signal spreading through a social network and fading at its edges"
              width="600"
              height="600"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </figure>
          <div class="entry-copy">
            <p><strong class="entry-title">The Life of a Signal</strong></p>
            <p>
              How long does a signal stay alive once people begin to lose interest? Here a product launch, a public-health message, and an online
              rumor all enter the same three-state nonlinear network. You can play the model, bend its conditions, and watch attention spread, peak,
              and fade. No signal was harmed in the simulation; most were eventually ignored.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-meta">Nonlinear dynamics · Social networks · Interactive visualisation</p>
            <p class="entry-links">
              <a class="quiet-link" href="{{ '/projects/the-life-of-a-signal/' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Site</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="content-row media-right">
          <div class="entry-copy">
            <p>
              <strong class="entry-title">Modelling Floral Pattern Formation in Yeast Biofilms</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
              Why does a yeast colony sometimes grow petals instead of a boring circle? Here we put two published models side by side:
              reaction–diffusion to ask when a smooth edge becomes unstable and picks a petal spacing, and continuum mechanics to track how cells,
              extracellular matrix, and fluid set the colony’s radius and speed. No yeast was asked to solve PDEs; it still left us plenty of them.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-links">
              <a
                class="quiet-link"
                href="{{ '/assets/pdf/modelling-floral-pattern-formation-yeast-biofilms-poster.pdf' | relative_url }}"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
                >Poster ↗</a
              >
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="square-media project-art yeast-biofilm-cover">
            <img
              src="{{ '/assets/img/yeast-biofilm-floral-cover-v2.jpg' | relative_url }}?v=20260722"
              alt="Scientific illustration of a yeast biofilm forming a radial floral boundary"
              width="600"
              height="600"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </figure>
        </article>

